title: Indonesian-Dutch Conference over the Fate of West Irian
subtitle: The long-awaited conference has begun!
new-page: true
tags: event
max-visits: 1
view-if: year = 1950 and month = 12

= The Fate of West Irian

<div><img src="img/round_table_conference_closing.jpg" alt="RTC" style="width: 75%;display: block;margin-top: 5px;margin: auto;margin-bottom: 5px;border: 5px solid var(--card-border-color);"></div>

In 1949, the Round Table Conference in the Hague concluded with The Netherlands granting sovereignty over the rest of the Dutch East Indies to the United States of Indonesia with the Republic as a constituent, ending the armed conflict in the archipelago. Furthermore, the agreement established the Netherlands-Indonesia Union, a loose, symbolic union connecting Indonesia and the Netherlands under the Dutch Crown. The Dutch delegation also agreed to withdraw Dutch troops "within the shortest possible time" and in turn the Indonesian delegation agreed to take over trade agreements negotiated by the Dutch East Indies and to ease economic relations between the two states. 

However disagreements came over two major issues: the debts of the Dutch colonial administration and the status of Western Irian. In particular our delegations were indignant at having to cover what we saw as the costs of Dutch military action against us and insists that Indonesia should comprise the entirety of the former Dutch East Indies. Eventually, with the intervention of the <span style="color: #B31942;">United</span> <span style="color: #0A3161;">States</span> member of the <span style="color: #009EDB;">UN</span> Commission on Indonesia, the Indonesian side agreed to pay part of the former colonial government's debt.

Therefore one topic remains: the fate of Western Irian. On one hand, the Indonesian delegation emphasizes the inclusion of the entire territory of the Dutch East Indies into Indonesia. On the other hand, the Dutch argues that Western Irian had no ethnic ties with the rest of the archipelago. The issue almost resulted in the talks becoming deadlocked, with either sides refusing to back down. 

Finally, on 1 November 1949, a compromise was reached: the status of Western Irian would be determined through negotiations between the United States of Indonesia and the Netherlands within a year of the transfer of sovereignty. Both sides had quarrels over the agreement, but eventually the Dutch Parliament and the KNIP ratified it on 21 and 14 December, respectively. Sovereignty was transferred to the United States of Indonesia on 27 December.

Now the one-year clause is nearing its end and the issue of Western Irian remains in the air.